# JFP for AQP: Implementation Log

## Root Cause Analysis (DONE)

### JOIN_FILTER_PUSHDOWN Asymmetry

Experimentally verified (see `duckdb_152_3_passes_analysis.md`): We isolated
3 candidate optimizer changes from 1.3.2->1.5.2, disabled them individually, and
measured. **JOIN_FILTER_PUSHDOWN accounts for 99.8% of the asymmetry.**

| Config | none-split (s) | node-based (s) | Gap (s) | Gap (%) |
|--------|---------------:|---------------:|--------:|--------:|
| All passes ON (baseline 1.5.2) | 9.682 | 10.716 | +1.035 | +10.7% |
| JFP only OFF | 15.732 | 14.416 | -1.316 | -8.4% |

| Metric | Value |
|--------|------:|
| none-split improvement from JFP | +6.050s |
| node-based improvement from JFP | +3.700s |
| Asymmetry (none - node) | +2.350s |

Disabling JFP flips the gap: node-based goes from 1.035s slower to 1.316s faster.

### What JFP Does (DuckDB 1.5.2)

The `JoinFilterPushdownOptimizer` sets up runtime dynamic filters pushed from hash
join build side to probe-side table scans. Three filter types at runtime:
- **Min/Max range filters** -- push min/max from build-side keys to probe-side scans,
  enabling zone-map-based row group skipping in storage
- **IN filters** (NEW in 1.5.2) -- for small hash tables, push exact value lists
- **Bloom filters** (NEW in 1.5.2) -- for medium hash tables, push probabilistic filters
- **SelectivityOptionalFilter** (NEW) -- adaptive wrapper that monitors selectivity
  and disables ineffective filters mid-execution

### What Changed: JFP 1.3.2 -> 1.5.2

| Feature | 1.3.2 | 1.5.2 | Impact |
|---------|-------|-------|--------|
| Min/Max range filters | Yes (simple) | Yes + SelectivityOptionalFilter wrapper | Adaptive: disables if selectivity > 75% |
| IN filters | Yes (small HTs) | Yes + storage type casting | More robust type handling |
| Bloom filters | No | Yes (medium HTs, single-key equality) | New row-level probabilistic filtering |
| SelectivityOptionalFilter | No | Yes | Makes aggressive pushdown safe |
| Cast-through pushdown | No | Yes (integral casts) | More join conditions eligible |
| Build-side filter detection | No | Yes (`IsFiltering()`) | Bloom filter only when build is filtered |

### Why Node-Based Loses the JFP Benefit: Three Gaps

#### Gap 1: No JFP pushdown TO temp table scans (code-verified)

The JFP optimizer's `GetPushdownFilterTargets()` only recognizes `LOGICAL_GET`
(type 25). Temp tables use `LogicalColumnDataGet` = `LOGICAL_CHUNK_GET` (type 26),
which falls through to the `default` no-op. Three-layer block:

1. Optimizer: No case for `LOGICAL_CHUNK_GET` in `GetPushdownFilterTargets()`
2. Type system: `PushdownFilterTarget` struct requires `LogicalGet&` reference
3. Physical operator: `PhysicalColumnDataScan` has no `dynamic_filters` member

#### Gap 2: Cross-boundary filter loss (inherent to splitting, dominant gap)

In none-split, filters from ANY join can reach ANY base table scan in the full
pipeline. In node-based, a base table scanned in subquery N cannot receive filters
from joins in subquery N+1.

#### Gap 3: No zone maps on ColumnDataCollection (inherent, NOT fixable)

ColumnDataCollection has no zone maps. Range filters degrade to row-by-row.
SelectivityOptionalFilter would detect low selectivity and auto-disable.
Bloom and IN filters work at row level regardless. ~5% of asymmetry.

#### Gap Impact Ranking (pre-measurement estimate)

| Gap | Estimated Impact | Fixable? |
|-----|-----------------|----------|
| Gap 2: cross-boundary | ~60-70% of asymmetry | Partially |
| Gap 1: temp as target | ~25-35% of asymmetry | Yes |
| Gap 3: no zone maps | ~5% of asymmetry | No |

